xnu 0493: let mondrianwifinorsoc skip the whole host reset

Measured 2026-11-27: the odd BHI session changes nothing (`0xef0a0111`,
`status=0`). The working SM8475 QCA6490 driver powers the chip, trains the
link and loads the firmware without any hot reset, LTSSM pulse, QFPROM
write, warm-entry clear or SOC global reset. The old `mondrianwifinorsoc`
returned after all of those, so its A/B never tested the cold chip. Return
at the top of `wifi_soc_reset` instead; the boot argument now selects the
whole no-reset path.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/mondrian_wifi.c b/bsd/dev/mondrian_wifi.c
--- a/bsd/dev/mondrian_wifi.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/mondrian_wifi.c
@@ -1339,6 +1339,24 @@
 	unsigned int i;
 
 	/*
+	 * A cold QCA6490 is already sitting in its boot ROM when the link
+	 * trains. The writes below are what ath11k sends to a device another
+	 * OS has booted, and the working SM8475 driver does none of them before
+	 * the BHI load. `mondrianwifinorsoc=1` skips the whole block so the
+	 * doorbell lands in the ROM the power-on left running.
+	 */
+	{
+		int skip = 0;
+
+		if (PE_parse_boot_argn("mondrianwifinorsoc", &skip,
+		    sizeof(skip)) && skip != 0) {
+			wifi_log("W6 reset skipped (mondrianwifinorsoc)");
+			mondrian_log_flush_now();
+			return 0;
+		}
+	}
+
+	/*
 	 * ath11k_pci_sw_reset(power_on=true), in its order. The LTSSM enable and
 	 * the hot-reset pulse come first: without them the SoC global reset
 	 * leaves the endpoint's PBL unclocked, which is the state 0442 measured
@@ -1380,23 +1398,6 @@
 	(void)mondrian_mmio_try_wr32(wifi_bar + 0x394, 0);
 	(void)mondrian_mmio_try_wr32(wifi_bar + 0x39c, 0);
 	mondrian_log_flush_now();
